WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
Speaker 1: OK. We have really substantive&nbsp;
follow up to a major story from either last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.880
week or the week before, not too long ago,&nbsp;
you might remember that I reported to you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:15.440
that two prosecutors working on the Manhattan&nbsp;
District Attorney's Criminal Probe of Donald&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:20.320
Trump's organization resigned. They just they&nbsp;
said, we quit. We're not doing it anymore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.440
and we went that story. This was from&nbsp;
OK, February 23rd, so two weeks ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:33.360
And in this original reporting, two prosecutors&nbsp;
resigned. This was a years old probe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.520
We didn't know why. We, as far as was&nbsp;
announced, the investigation was still ongoing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:45.600
and it was sort of a real question mark. And at&nbsp;
the time, my speculation remember speculation was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:52.960
these prosecutors realized. They're not going to&nbsp;
indict Trump. They're not going to charge Trump.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:58.240
And they felt my work is being done in vain.&nbsp;
I'm getting the hell out of here. That was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:05.840
my speculation. And I identified it as such. To&nbsp;
be clear, the speculation on the right was they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:11.600
quit because they knew there was no evidence&nbsp;
against the Trump Organization and they felt they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:18.640
were being railroaded into. There was no evidence&nbsp;
of that. There was no evidence of my hypothesis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:26.320
but I argued to you that my hypothesis is far&nbsp;
more plausible and my hypothesis has now been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:34.800
vindicated and vindicated. Exactly. Prosecutors&nbsp;
resigned, says a new article after New York D.A.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:40.960
said he wasn't prepared to move forward with&nbsp;
the indictment of Donald Trump. We now know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:47.280
the reason, and I got lucky, it happened&nbsp;
to be the reason I said to top prosecutors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.160
leading the criminal probe into Trump and his&nbsp;
business resigned after the Manhattan district&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:56.960
attorney said he was not prepared to authorize&nbsp;
an indictment against the former president,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:03.120
a person familiar with the investigation said.&nbsp;
And there it is. Kerry Dunn and Mark Pomerantz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.760
two senior prosecutors on the team, resigned last&nbsp;
month, one day after Manhattan District Attorney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:12.560
Alvin Bragg informed them he's not prepared&nbsp;
to move forward with criminal charges. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.480
resignations followed weeks of internal debate&nbsp;
and discussion over the strength of evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.840
and whether it could pass the hurdle of proving a&nbsp;
crime. Now I want to be really clear it's possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:28.880
that the D.A. is correct. We don't have enough&nbsp;
to prove a Trump crime here. That may well be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:32.960
but the question was why would they resign?&nbsp;
Would they resign? Because there's nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:37.360
here to investigate. Doesn't seem likely because&nbsp;
they've been investigating for a long time. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:41.520
that was the reason they would have known, there&nbsp;
was no evidence a long time ago. Or is it more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:45.760
likely that they were getting frustrated with the&nbsp;
fact that they've been investigating for so long?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:52.000
They have what they might see as a solid case&nbsp;
built up. And yet no one is willing to indict.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:58.320
And that appears to be it. Now, interestingly,&nbsp;
there's some detail here about why this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:05.760
the abrupt resignation. OK, where's the section&nbsp;
here? Was originally started by Cy Vance. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:10.640
know that the New York Times first reported that&nbsp;
the resignations followed a decision by Bragg to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:15.200
start presenting evidence to the grand jury. The&nbsp;
strength of the evidence against Trump has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:20.160
debated by attorneys in the office for months.&nbsp;
Some prosecutors, including the two that resigned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.040
believed there was sufficient evidence to charge&nbsp;
Trump, while others, including career prosecutors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:29.520
were skeptical they could get a conviction because&nbsp;
of the difficulty in proving criminal intent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:34.560
That's another thing we've talked about a lot of&nbsp;
the crimes here that Donald Trump could be charged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:41.200
with really require you to show what his state&nbsp;
of mind and intent was. And that can be very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:46.320
very difficult. On the one hand, some prosecutors&nbsp;
believe Trump speaks with a lot of hyperbole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.320
but it wasn't clear that they could&nbsp;
show he acted with intent to defraud.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:54.800
There's also a very important&nbsp;
piece here about Michael Cohen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:00.080
and this I think we should talk about a&nbsp;
little more. Multiple factors were at play,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:05.600
including lack of key Trump Organization insider&nbsp;
cooperating with insiders cooperating with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.640
investigation, who at trial could walk the jury&nbsp;
through the evidence. The possible value of using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:16.560
Michael Cohen, Trump's former personal attorney,&nbsp;
who in testimony before Congress alleged Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:23.440
inflated and devalued assets for his advantage,&nbsp;
was discussed with the team. Cohen pleaded guilty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:28.640
to nine charges, including lying to Congress. He&nbsp;
was once close to Trump, but his conviction and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:34.240
public statements critical of Trump would give&nbsp;
the defense ammunition to aim to discredit him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:41.600
That is a very, very interesting piece of this.&nbsp;
The idea is if you you don't have a cooperating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:46.720
witness from inside Trump's business, you do&nbsp;
have all sorts of claims from Michael Cohen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:51.360
But Michael Cohen, if you read between the lines,&nbsp;
what they're saying is would be easy to discredit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:59.520
and destroy his credibility in a cross-examination&nbsp;
on the basis that he has perjured himself. He has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:05.200
lied. He has a long history of all of the&nbsp;
trouble that ultimately got him convicted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:12.880
The counter to that be. Well, those are all crimes&nbsp;
he committed for Donald Trump. But I think it's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:19.440
very legitimate question to say what is a jury&nbsp;
going to think if Michael Cohen is their main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:24.880
witness and is cross-examined about all the times&nbsp;
that he lied? Well, so you told the lie, then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:29.200
how are we to believe that you are telling the&nbsp;
truth now, et cetera, et cetera? So the point here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:34.800
is this is number one exactly what we believe was&nbsp;
going on prosecutors getting sick of the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:40.160
they're doing their work. And nobody really seems&nbsp;
willing to prosecute. That seems to be going on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.920
And then number two, this is why I continue&nbsp;
to say Donald Trump is not going to spend a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:49.840
single day in prison, and I apologize to people&nbsp;
who think I'm causing that to be the truth. No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:54.880
prosecutors are listening to me. They're going to&nbsp;
decide what to do, regardless of what I say. I see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.880 --> 00:06:02.880
it as a very high bar to reach that. A prosecutor&nbsp;
will say, I'm going to be the first. The first to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:10.240
indict a former president. I think if one did it,&nbsp;
others would fall. But who is willing to be first?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:16.400
We haven't found anybody quite yet. And that's the&nbsp;
latest with the prosecution that's in Manhattan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:21.280
There's Georgia Southern District of New York.&nbsp;
There's all these other things going on, but at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:26.560
least as far as what's going on in Manhattan,&nbsp;
a logical follow up to the story from February.

